On Oct 10, 2007, at 5:01 AM, A. Pagaltzis wrote:

> Hi,
>
>>     # Hope nobody defines a sub by this name
>>     sub UNIVERSAL::a_sub_not_likely_to_be_here { ref($_[0]) }
>>
>>     sub blessed ($) {
>>       local($@, $SIG{__DIE__}, $SIG{__WARN__});
>>       length(ref($_[0]))
>>         ? eval { $_[0]->a_sub_not_likely_to_be_here }
>>         : undef
>>     }
>
> is there any reason to pollute UNIVERSAL here? Shouldn’t the
> following work identically?
>
>     sub blessed ($) {
>       local($@, $SIG{__DIE__}, $SIG{__WARN__});
>       length(ref($_[0]))
>         ? eval { $_[0]->can('can') && $_[0] }
>         : undef
>     }

No it will not. What if the class of $_[0] has over-ridden can ?  
However, you can call $_[0]->UNIVERSAL::can('can') to avoid that

Graham.

